  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Regional CSO Anti-Corruption Coalition Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID Central America Regional):  This activity will build capacity of local civil society organizations (CSOs) that are part of the Center against Corruption and Impunity in Northern Central America (CCINOC), a regional consortium. The activity will develop CCINOC&apos;s ability to foster cross-border cooperation, facilitate resource and knowledge sharing, and drive citizen engagement and advocacy. This will improve CCINOC&apos;s administration, governance, and technical capacity for coordinating activities. Over the course of the five-year activity CCINOC will expand its partner base and network, and seek opportunities for private sector engagement. This may include engagement with organizations based in Belize, Costa Rica, and Panama including those representing Nicaraguan diaspora groups. The activity will establish CCINOC as a regional hub for knowledge sharing, cooperation, and development of talent, technical tools, and expertise; a central location for cooperation with international actors; and a catalyst for citizen-led advocacy for accountability toward corruption. CCINOC will focus on three key areas: raising awareness on corruption, impunity, and democratic backsliding at local, regional, and international levels; enhancing CSOs&apos; ability to document corruption; and increasing public engagement in a regional anti-corruption agenda. CCINOC will seek to engage the private sector to improve business integrity, advance transparency, and contribute to anticorruption efforts. The activity contributes to the Regional Development Cooperation Strategy (RDCS) Regional Development Objective (RDO) 2, &quot;Strengthen Regional Responses to Corruption and Citizen Security;&quot; supports Pillar II of the Root Causes Strategy (RCS), &quot;Combating corruption, strengthening democratic governance, and advancing the rule of law;&quot; and advances Pillars III and V of the U.S. Strategy on Countering Corruption.</narrative>
  </description>
